    "result_text": "All 63 tests pass. Let me confirm the cap actually engages (not just a no-op) with a quick sanity check of the audit output showing capped levels from the earlier household run \u2014 Cp23/Cp39 logged `membershipLevel=3` for the third member while the cap allowed exactly `max(2)+1=3`, confirming the ceiling is computed against the household's current standing.\n\nThe change is complete. Here's what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` may not exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their existing household members; with no existing member, no cap applies.\n\n`membershipLevel` was previously a purely derived value (`Membership.levelOf`) computed in `OwnerMapper` at response time \u2014 with no access to the household, and no way to persist a capped result across the create\u2192GET round-trip. So I made it a computed-then-stored field:\n\n- **`Owner`** \u2014 added a persisted `membershipLevel` (`Integer`, column `membership_level`) with getter/setter, and the column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb is the one tests use).\n- **`Membership`** \u2014 added `pointsOf(owner, householdMemberCount)` / `levelOf(owner, householdMemberCount)` and a public `levelForPoints`, so an existing member can be scored against the household's *current* size rather than the stale count stored when they were created.\n- **`AssignMembershipLevel`** (new pipeline step) \u2014 computes the owner's natural level, then caps it at `maxExistingHouseholdMemberLevel + 1` (each existing member re-scored at the current household size); no existing member \u2192 no cap. Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after `countNamesakes`/`assignHouseholdSize` (which supply the inputs) and before `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** and **`AuditOwnerCreated`** \u2014 now emit the stored capped level, falling back to the computed level when null (e.g. seed data).\n\n## One thing to flag\n\nCp51 (the new test) failed on its **precondition**, not the cap: it created a second owner in the same household (same lastName+postcode) *without* `sharesHousehold: true`, which the household-duplicate rule rejects with 409. Cp10 and Cp35 hard-require that exact 409, so the identical payload cannot also succeed \u2014 the test as delivered was internally inconsistent with the existing suite. Every other multi-member household test (Cp11, Cp23, Cp35, Cp36, Cp39) sets `sharesHousehold: true`; Cp51 omitted it. I added that one line to Cp51 so the second owner is a declared household member, without weakening the rule the other tests depend on. If you'd prefer I not touch the test, the alternative would be an unprincipled change to the duplicate rule (letting an email-bearing owner bypass the household block), which I'd advise against.\n\nFull suite: **63 passed, 0 failed.**",
